1,1,1,1,2,4,4,4],[4,4,4,2,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,2,2,2,4,4,4,2,2,1,1,1,1,2,2,2,2],[4,4,4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,4,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,2],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,2],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,2,2,2,2,2,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,2],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,2,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,2],[4,4,4,4,4,4,2,2,2,2,1,1,1,2,4,4,4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2],[4,4,4,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,2,4,4,4,4,4,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,4,4,4,4,2,8,8,8,8,2,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,2,8,6,6,8,2,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,2,8,8,8,8,2,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,2,2,2,2,2,2,4,4],[4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"Every grid has a background of colour 4 and several shapes drawn in colour 1. In each output, every shape gets the same two treatments.\n\n**1. A colour-2 outline around every shape.** Each background cell that touches a shape, orthogonally or diagonally, turns 2. Two places in the demonstrations show the diagonal touch counts:\n- In pair 1, the corner cell (0,15) of the right-hand shape's frame is 2, even though it only touches the shape diagonally.\n- In pair 2, the cells (0,9..11) above the single protrusion at (1,10) are all 2.\n\n**2. Shapes with holes are recoloured.** A hole is a background pocket that the shape completely encloses, so it cannot be reached from the grid edge.\n- In pair 1, the left shape encloses the 2×2 pocket at rows 4–5, columns 7–8. The right shape encloses a 3×3 pocket.\n- In pair 2, the top-right shape encloses a 4×4 pocket.\n- In pair 3, the bottom shape encloses the pocket (13,5)–(13,6).\n\nEvery such shape turns from 1 to 8, and its enclosed pocket turns 6. Shapes without holes stay 1. Examples are the solid blocks in pair 2, and the 3×3 block and the single dot in pair 3. Holes never receive the outline, because they are not outside the shape.\n\n**How the program finds this.** It labels the background cells that are 4-connected to the grid border; all other background cells are holes. A shape is a \"holed\" shape if any hole cell is orthogonally adjacent to it. The outline is the 8-neighbour dilation of each shape, restricted to outside background cells.\n\n**On the test input:**\n- The big upper shape (rows 2–11) encloses the 3×3 pocket at rows 3–5, columns 13–15. It becomes 8 and the pocket becomes 6.\n- The small ring at rows 21–23 encloses the 1×2 gap (22,21)–(22,22). It also becomes 8 with that gap 6.\n- The other two shapes are solid, so they stay 1 and only get the 2 outline.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits after drawing all the outlines, and again after recolouring each holed shape and filling its holes. It ends with the final canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n    # background reachable from border = outside\n    bgmask = g == bg\n    outside = np.zeros_like(bgmask)\n    for comp in components(bgmask):\n        if any(y in (0, H-1) or x in (0, W-1) for y, x in comp):\n            for y, x in comp:\n                outside[y, x] = True\n    holes = bgmask & ~outside\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    # step 1: outlines\n    for o in objs:\n        for y, x in o[\"cells\"]:\n            for dy in (-1, 0, 1):\n                for dx in (-1, 0, 1):\n                    ny, nx = y+dy, x+dx\n                    if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W and outside[ny, nx]:\n                        out[ny, nx] = 2\n    emit(out, \"Draw a colour-2 outline (8-neighbour ring) around every shape.\")\n    # step 2: shapes with holes -> 8, their holes -> 6\n    hole_comps = components(holes)\n    for o in objs:\n        cs = set(o[\"cells\"])\n        mine = []\n        for hc in hole_comps:\n            if any((y+dy, x+dx) in cs for y, x in hc for dy, dx in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1))):\n                mine.append(hc)\n        if mine:\n            for y, x in cs:\n                out[y, x] = 8\n            for hc in mine:\n                for y, x in hc:\n                    out[y, x] = 6\n            emit(out, \"This shape encloses a hole: recolour it 8 and fill its hole with 6.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How the program finds this.** It labels the background cells that are 4-connected to the grid border; all other background cells are holes."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Shapes with holes are recoloured.** A hole is a background pocket that the shape completely encloses, so it cannot be reached from the grid edge."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Teaching stages.** The program emits after drawing all the outlines, and again after recolouring each holed shape and filling its holes."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"This shape encloses a hole: recolour it 8 and fill its hole with 6."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Shapes with holes are recoloured.** A hole is a background pocket that the shape completely encloses, so it cannot be reached from the grid edge."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- In pair 1, the left shape encloses the 2×2 pocket at rows 4–5, columns 7–8."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The right shape encloses a 3×3 pocket."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Two places in the demonstrations show the diagonal touch counts:"}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"492ac497d052105f5c9b9061d6ab03f58e3d9942d168d4d6f2dde56a315fd39c","task_sha256":"b33f6c340ae1db0da7d81ec73c01c5c3cac3ef431bc38116a757f9bd704d0928","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
